{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.CrystalBasisResolvesMassTowerOpenProblems","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.CrystalBasisResolvesMassTowerOpenProblems","imports":["lean:module:Init"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.crystal_basis_reduction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.crystal_basis_reduction","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L108-L131","doc":"**Crystal-basis correspondence**\n([`prop:crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems`]).\n\nGiven the three reductions, discharging the three finite crystal-graph\ncomputations settles three of the four open mass-tower problems.\n\nDerived: the conclusion is none of the class's fields, and the proof is the\nthree implications applied to the three computations.  This is what a\nreduction claim is *for*, and it is the part the previous encoding — a bare\n`Prop` field handed back — did not state.\n\nThe fourth problem does not appear in the conclusion, because nothing here\nreduces it. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems","label":"prop:crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.zHabiro_reduces","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.zHabiro_reduces","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L106-L106","doc":"The Habiro convergence divergence reduces to the Kazhdan–Lusztig\nclassical-limit functor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.term7_reduces","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.term7_reduces","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L103-L103","doc":"The Term 7 shell correction reduces to enumerating crystal vertices at\ndepth 6. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.chirality_reduces","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.chirality_reduces","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L100-L100","doc":"The chirality factor average reduces to an integer multiplicity sum on\na Type-B folded crystal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence.mk","kind":"ctor","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L95-L95","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.CrystalBasisCorrespondence","kind":"class","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L80-L106","doc":"The crystal-basis correspondence, as a class over the four problems and\nthe three finite computations it concerns.\n\n`chirality`, `term7`, `zHabiro` and `blochGroup` are the four open mass-tower\nproblems; `chiralityCount`, `term7DepthSix` and `zHabiroKLLimit` are the\nthree finite crystal-graph computations the block names.  All seven are\n**parameters**, so the class is a hypothesis about the caller's propositions.\n\nThe three fields are the conjecture, conditional on Brundan–Kleshchev and\nLecouvey.  No instance is supplied.  `blochGroup` is carried so the fourth\nproblem is visible in the type and its absence from the fields is legible:\nthe class makes no claim about it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ReducesTo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ReducesTo","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-basis-resolves-mass-tower-open-problems.lean#L71-L78","doc":"**`problem` reduces to `computation`**: carrying out the computation\nsettles the problem.\n\nA reduction is an implication in this direction and not the other; stating it\nthe other way round would say that the problem being open bounds the\ncomputation, which is not what a reduction claims. ","signatureChunk":0}]}